WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.840
Conventionally speaking, aggro is a fairly&nbsp;
straightforward archetype. Your opponent is at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:10.280
a life total. The rules of the game say you need&nbsp;
to turn that life total to zero. You try to do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:16.160
as quickly as possible. Common ways of doing&nbsp;
this consist of direct damage, small creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.600
large creatures rushed out quickly, and so&nbsp;
forth. Red Deck Wins is instructive here:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.600
a mixture of burn spells and small creatures&nbsp;
means that the aggro player will try to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.040
as much creature damage as possible early&nbsp;
and then hope that they can run the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.480
to the finish line with burn. In a 20 health&nbsp;
format, this is straightforward and makes sense.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:41.360
What about in commander though? That 120&nbsp;
health format we all know and love. A mere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.200
39 lightning bolts are needed, presuming&nbsp;
each player gutshots themself with a land.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:52.360
Or 39 swings with a Delver of Secrets, if you&nbsp;
prefer. Or 13 swings with an elite squad of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:58.800
three Delvers of Secret. Hmmm. Perplexing. The&nbsp;
answer is that it doesn’t work. The fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.080
EDH is a 40 life format is a pain point for our&nbsp;
hypothetical red-deck-winner. With this in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.560
I think an instructive exercise is to&nbsp;
think about what that 40 life really means.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:17.800
First of all, lifepay is so much less punishing.&nbsp;
Fetches and shocks? Easy. Phyrexian mana symbols?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:23.040
Close enough to free. Paying 1 life for&nbsp;
1 card? An even more delicious bargain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.600
than usual. These effects, worthwhile but&nbsp;
noticeably painful in a 20 health format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:34.880
are now nearly painless. Secondly, blocking&nbsp;
isn’t as necessary. Decks are empowered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.320
constantly use tap abilities, to swing in&nbsp;
for value with a Bident of Thassa effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:44.680
and to eat 5-10 damage hits rather than blocking&nbsp;
with an important creature. They’re also empowered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:51.240
to spend their early turns throwing down snowbally&nbsp;
value cards rather than committing much to board.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.160
So, with that stuff in mind—what happens if&nbsp;
you remove that extra health and take away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:02.520
those things? Suddenly that Phyrexian Arena is a&nbsp;
liability. That Dismember is close to regaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.920
its status as a three mana black card. Your&nbsp;
commander might need to be a blocker if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:12.400
didn’t add enough creatures to your deck, and&nbsp;
trading is the best case scenario. Spending a turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.120
playing setup cards for an approaching big turn&nbsp;
goes from an exciting proposition to a terrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.120
one when you might be the one to get knocked out&nbsp;
if you skimp on blockers. Your hands are tied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:27.080
your knees are quivering, and your deck&nbsp;
decidedly was not built for this situation.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:32.560
To me, this is the best starting point for looking&nbsp;
at EDH aggro. Your goal doesn’t start and end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.560
with reducing your opponent’s life total to zero&nbsp;
like it generally does in 20 health 1v1 formats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.000
because cutting out a large chunk of an&nbsp;
opponent’s life total is helpful in its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.440
own right. My mantra in a game where somebody&nbsp;
has a deck that is concerning and unpredictable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:52.320
but not necessarily totally dominant, is that they&nbsp;
should be at 15 life. When somebody is at 15 life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.640
they have to play much more conservatively.&nbsp;
Having safe setup turns isn’t a given,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.720
and they’re generally one round of attacks away&nbsp;
from death if they start doing concerning things.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:08.200
Going forward, I’m going to refer to this concept&nbsp;
as Aggro Ideology. This is not the beginning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:13.760
end of what aggro means in EDH, but simply&nbsp;
a phrase I use to gesture at the benefits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.560
having opponents who are scared about their life&nbsp;
total. If I had to pick out a second feature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.720
the commander format that’s important for aggro&nbsp;
decks, it’s commander damage. If your commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:29.120
deals 21 combat damage to an opponent, they lose.&nbsp;
This means that if your deck is commander based,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.600
you’re playing a 21 health format instead of the&nbsp;
much more challenging 40 health format. Neat! Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.480
aggro ideology and commander damage aren’t&nbsp;
mutually exclusive, nor do you need both.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.400
For a summary of what decks care about&nbsp;
what things, this is a handy diagram.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.640
Outside the circles, we have decks that&nbsp;
lose. There may be effective aggro decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.600
that don’t use commander damage and don’t take&nbsp;
advantage of disrupted opponents, but they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:59.840
exceedingly rare. On the right here, we have pure&nbsp;
voltron. This is a deck that plays a commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.240
buffs it and protects it through a combination&nbsp;
of other cards, and kills players very quickly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:10.680
large bursts of damage. The higher level an aggro&nbsp;
list is, the more voltron-y it will be, generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:16.560
speaking. In the middle here is pseudo-voltron.&nbsp;
These decks will commonly utilize commander damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.480
to win, but will often have other forms of damage&nbsp;
as well. This gives the deck multiple win plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.040
as well as allowing for both focused kills and&nbsp;
general table beatdown, which may have different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.880
use-cases depending on what you’re playing&nbsp;
against. The last type of deck here is disruptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:37.360
aggro. This is a deck that fully commits to the&nbsp;
plan of making its opponents uncomfortable. You’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:42.960
commonly see lots of cards that damage multiple&nbsp;
opponents, often paired with some form of stax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.560
I’ve included links to examples to each of&nbsp;
these types of decks in the description.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.400
Now that I’ve laid out a conceptual foundation&nbsp;
for aggro, let’s talk about the specific goals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:55.520
and challenges that are important within&nbsp;
a game. For a creature-based aggro deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.240
the basic idea is simple: you want creatures&nbsp;
on board, and you want them to connect with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:04.720
your opponents’ life totals. To connect with life&nbsp;
totals, you need to get past blockers. The most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:10.360
common way of going about this is evasion, either&nbsp;
innately on the aggressive creatures or applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:16.120
through equipments or cards such as Wonder. An&nbsp;
alternative method is eliminating those blockers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.600
but this is much less common—one example is&nbsp;
a budget Rakdos discard deck I built that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:25.560
runs Malfegor as the commander mostly&nbsp;
as a way of clearing out blockers.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.880
Maintaining creatures on board is a separate&nbsp;
challenge, with the roadblock there usually being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:35.760
boardwipes. There’s no one foolproof solution to&nbsp;
this, but here are some potential workarounds.&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.680
For one, be faster than the deck&nbsp;
with the boardwipes, and target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:43.920
them first. This is likely the preferred&nbsp;
method for higher power voltron decks.&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:47.560
Alternatively run protection&nbsp;
spells—permanent-based is preferable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.040
for an aggro deck, with Selfless Spirit&nbsp;
being the absolute gold standard if you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.120
running white. You can also run instants that&nbsp;
protect your creatures, or even counter-spells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:59.880
but keep in mind that holding up mana will make&nbsp;
your deck less mana efficient unless you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:06.240
treasures or unless your creatures have flash.
As a third option, run Blood Artist effects—this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:12.120
is my preferred strategy when playing black, as&nbsp;
it ties in beautifully with our Aggro Ideology.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.760
The point about outrunning boardwipes ties&nbsp;
into a broader point about the speed of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:20.000
deck. Speed and resilience are factors&nbsp;
that need to be considered—a deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.200
can outrun most combos and kill midrange&nbsp;
before it stabilizes might not need much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.440
in the way of counterplays or resilience&nbsp;
to obstacles, but a slower deck might want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:33.240
a lot more of those things. However, putting&nbsp;
those things in a deck also tends to slow it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:38.840
down more, so it’s a balancing act. My Liesa&nbsp;
hatebears deck is a good example of slower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:43.720
more resilient aggro deck that is constantly&nbsp;
chewing on life totals but whose reliably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:50.280
brutal commander and high density of stax mean&nbsp;
it doesn’t necessarily need to win super early.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:55.200
Past the basics, a key skill you’ll want&nbsp;
to develop is target prioritization. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.400
your deck prefers to kill one player at a&nbsp;
time, you need to pick a player to focus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.960
and picking wrong might lose you the game.&nbsp;
Primary targets that come to mind are players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.440
who pay a lot of life, and players who have&nbsp;
especially strong ways to shut you down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:12.480
but it’s definitely a vibes-based thing and when&nbsp;
in doubt you can just attack the opponent you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:18.400
most afraid of. A final challenge I’ll mention&nbsp;
is that at some tables, aggro is considered rude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.920
in some capacity. This is especially common&nbsp;
in pods populated with fiddly midrange decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.600
where knocking a player out early means&nbsp;
they’ll spend upwards of an hour sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.000
on the sidelines. These environments&nbsp;
are not my preferred one to play in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.400
but ultimately they simply stem from the&nbsp;
fact that EDH is a social game played in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.440
a lot of different ways. The kiddie gloves&nbsp;
fiddly version of it is one way it’s played,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.240
but you can certainly try to smack a little&nbsp;
variety into your playgroup with a beatdown deck.

